    "title": "The regular pumping lemma · Understand the rule",
    "concept": "If L is regular, some p works for every word w in L of length at least p: w=xyz with |xy|≤p, |y|>0 and xy^iz in L for every i≥0.",
    "foundation": {
      "title": "The regular pumping lemma",
      "idea": "If L is regular, some p works for every word w in L of length at least p: w=xyz with |xy|≤p, |y|>0 and xy^iz in L for every i≥0.",
      "terms": [
        "Pumping length p: The hypothetical finite bound supplied by regularity.",
        "Decomposition xyz: A split with |xy|≤p and nonempty y.",
        "Pumping exponent i: A nonnegative number of copies of y.",
        "Repetition a^n: A sequence containing n copies of a; n is a nonnegative integer.",
        "Length |w|: The number of symbols in w; |xy|≤p bounds the combined length of x and y."
      ],
      "example": "For a^p b^p, every legal y lies in the a block. Pumping down removes a symbols but no b symbols."
    },
    "teachingSteps": [
      "If L is regular, some p works for every word w in L of length at least p: w=xyz with |xy|≤p, |y|>0 and xy^iz in L for every i≥0.",
      "For a^p b^p, every legal y lies in the a block. Pumping down removes a symbols but no b symbols.",
      "The opponent chooses the decomposition; showing failure for one convenient split is insufficient."
    ],
